Project Overview
The primary objective of this project is to explore and analyze household data to understand
socio-economic trends such as income distribution, family size, and demographics. The project
is split into two main sections: theory and practical application.

1. Theoretical Foundations
The theoretical part of the project defines the fundamental statistical concepts used during the
analysis.
Types of Data
Numerical Data: Data that represents quantities and can be measured. Examples include
Household Income, Age of Household Head, and Family Size.
Categorical Data: Data that represents characteristics or labels. Examples include
Education Level, Owns House, and Urban/Rural status.
Statistical Categories
Descriptive Statistics: Used to summarize and describe the features of a specific dataset
(e.g., Mean income, Median age).
Inferential Statistics: Used to make predictions or generalizations about a larger population
based on a sample of data.
Key Terms
Central Tendency: Mean (average), Median (middle value), and Mode (most frequent
value).
Dispersion: Range (Max-Min), Variance (average squared deviation), and Standard
Deviation (spread of data).
Distribution Shapes: Skewness (asymmetry) and Kurtosis (peakedness).

2. Practical Analysis
The analysis was performed using Python libraries such as Pandas, NumPy, and Matplotlib.
Below are the key steps and findings.
Data Inspection
The dataset was loaded and inspected to identify data types and ensure integrity. Numerical
columns identified include Age, Income, and Family Size, while Categorical columns include
Education and Location.
Statistical Summary
Household Income: Mean ≈ $86,583.21; Median = $93,892.50.
Age: Mean ≈ 47.09; Median = 48.0.
Income Spread: The income range is $132,140, indicating significant economic diversity
within the sample.
Visualizations
Visual aids such as histograms were used to observe the frequency distribution of income.
Boxplots were utilized to compare income across different education levels and geographic
areas (Urban vs. Rural).

3. Conclusion

The analysis successfully characterized the household dataset, highlighting a slightly left-
skewed income distribution and a strong correlation between education levels and family size/

income variance.
